      "content": "While social dynamics can indeed differ between generations, and it's possible that what you perceive as contemporary and relevant to a younger demographic might hold a negative connotation in local circles, including those of the delegates you're addressing, the risk of it coming across as offensive remains significant. Diplomatic contexts demand a high degree of sensitivity, and even within the younger generation, there can be varying sensibilities towards such references, especially if directed towards outsiders. Cultural expressions are subjective, and what you may perceive as a nod might still be seen as unprofessional or disrespectful by many within the delegation.\n\nRather than risking misinterpretation and potential offense, I recommend that you focus on common ground that doesn't involve culturally sensitive material. Familiarize yourself with positive, shared values and perhaps learn a few basic phrases in the language of the delegates ahead of time. This can show genuine interest and respect while steering clear of potential pitfalls. Cultivating mutual respect in all interactions is the best approach to building lasting rapport without relying on controversial references.",
